#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
"""lanes-index - the DERIVED INDEX of the lane register (lane-collision-protocol
Amendment 14, ratified by Brett Heap 2026-10-05, verbatim "ratify 48").

    lanes-index sync      [--source <s>]              write behind what LANDED
    lanes-index reconcile [--source <s>] [--dry-run]  read the source whole
    lanes-index status                                 per source: store, lag, errors
    lanes-index export    --out <dir> [--source <s> | --worktrees]
                                                       the read flags' reader

THE REGISTER'S TRUTH STAYS WHERE IT IS (clause (a)). The workspace repository's
`lanes/LANES.md`, `lanes/log/*.md`, `lanes/aliases.tsv` and `lanes/archive/`
are the record, written only by `lanes-edit.sh`. This file keeps a COPY of what
they say, in tables a question across lanes can be asked of, and makes nothing
else true: where the index and the source disagree, the source is right and the
index is behind.

NOTHING THAT ACTS READS IT (clause (b)). No claim, release, log line, guard,
hook, launch, row writer, sweep or migration reads this store or waits on it.
`lanes-edit.sh` starts `lanes-index sync` DETACHED after a push has landed and
its mutex is released - stdin closed, never waited, its status never read - and
two read-only words consult it behind a flag typed for that invocation:
`lanes ... --index` and `lanes-edit.sh history ... --index`, through `export`.

WRITE-BEHIND, BY THE INDEXER ALONE, NEVER BACK (clause (d)). `sync` reads the
register as `origin/<branch>` of the workspace checkout stands - what the push
left, never the working tree - and upserts each record keyed by (source, record
id) and versioned by a digest of its content: an upsert bearing the stored
digest changes nothing, so a replay or a crash is harmless. The source's
indexed commit is compared and swapped IN THE SAME TRANSACTION as the rows it
covers, and a sync from a commit that does not descend from it writes nothing.
Nothing in any source is ever written from here.

THE INVENTORY IS A SECOND SOURCE (opensoft/openRepoTools#161, the "later word"
clause (e) left open): `inventory:<Workstation>`, THIS workstation's #97
worktree inventory, copied into the `worktrees` table. It is read through
`lanes-edit.sh worktrees --all` - the sidecars, the lifecycle snapshot,
`managed-projection`'s owner and the listing's liveness, in the helper that
wrote them - and never through a parser of this file's own or a walk of the
disk. Its rows are keyed by (source, `<lane>:<path>`) - a second tree of one lane
with the same path `<lane>:<path><US><n>`, and a lane's unread marker
`<lane>:<US>unread` - and versioned by the same digest; a tree the inventory no
longer names leaves in the same sync. Its
provenance moves only forward PER LANE: a sync that reads a lane's lifecycle
generation below the one its rows were written at writes nothing for that lane
(`reconcile` takes the lane as it now stands, and says so); a lane whose
inventory could not be read keeps its rows and gets one marker
row, with no path and a branch no git ref can be (#173), which `export
--worktrees` writes back as the source's own `UNREAD` line; a read whose LANE
LISTING was not whole - the helper's `PARTIAL` line: its register, its archive,
the list of its logs or a log could not be read - writes nothing at all (#173
item c1), since a lane it could not name is not a lane with no trees; and the
source's own row carries a fingerprint of the whole table as its
compare-and-swap token and a sync generation that only counts up.
`lanes-edit.sh` nudges `sync --source inventory` after every inventory write.
Nothing that acts reads this table either: `lane-reconcile`, `lane-start`,
`lane-end` and the sweep read the sidecars and the disk.

THE STORE (clause (c)): QA Postgres where this workstation's configuration
names a `url=`, reached through `psql` and only then; otherwise one SQLite file
on local disk. The configuration is
`${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/openRepoTools/lanes-index.conf`, or the file
`$LANES_INDEX_CONFIG` names: `store=`, `url=`, `passfile=`, `sqlite=`, and for
the SELECT-only role `reader_url=`/`reader_passfile=`, and `schema=`. It is
refused when it is group- or world-readable, inside a git work tree or under
`~/.agents/`, and when a URL carries a password: the secret lives only in the
0600 libpq password file `passfile=` names. The reads (`status`, `export`)
connect to Postgres only as `reader_url=` and are refused without it - never
with the writer's credential.

Exit codes: 0 done · 1 a read or a write failed (nothing half-written) · 2 a
refusal (the configuration, a `--source` that is not this workstation's, an
inventory on a workstation with no name) · 3
`export` only: the index cannot answer (missing, unreachable, wiped, unknown
schema - the reason is in `<out>/why`) · 64 usage.
"""

